WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.934
-Man, oh, man.
I'm so happy to meet you.

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:04.734
And thank you so much
for coming on the show.

00:00:04.734 --> 00:00:06.467
You hit a home run
with this movie, buddy.

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:09.534
I can't even tell you
what a great job you did.

00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:11.400
We saw it.
We screened it the other night.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.767
You're fantastic in this movie.

00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:14.167
-Thank you.
-Congratulations.

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:16.133
-Thank you so much.
-It was so good.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:17.501
I want to get down to
all the details

00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:19.634
and ask you all the questions.

00:00:19.634 --> 00:00:21.667
Here's some of the reviews,
by the way, just so you know,

00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:23.033
if you want to plug your ears.

00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:26.934
But one says, "When
Austin Butler shakes his hips

00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:29.767
in Elvis' first gig as
a full-blown rock 'n' roller,

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:31.901
it's like watching
two stars being born,"

00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:34.634
which is -- That was "Time Out."

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:38.167
I like that one.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:40.300
"Vanity Fair" says,
"It may well be

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.434
a star-making turn
for Austin Butler.

00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:43.567
And I agree with that.

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:45.100
And then this one
is my favorite.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.467
"Entertainment Weekly" says,

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:50.300
"Austin Butler stares
down the lens and melts it."

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.033
That's -- I've never heard that,
melting it.

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:57.367
But I did feel that,
when I was watching the thing --

00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:59.534
Baz Luhrmann did
such an amazing job creating it.

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:01.434
-Oh, yeah, he did.

00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:03.167
I'm so proud of
everything he's done.

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:04.367
-Yeah.
-It was incredible.

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:06.000
-Did you feel
when you're auditioning --

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.300
When you knew that there was
an audition for Elvis

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.300
and you're like,
"I'm close to it,"

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.400
did you get nervous?

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.033
Did you always know Elvis?
-Yeah.

00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:13.934
-You did.
-Yeah, it's terrifying.

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:17.534
I mean, but it's that thing
where it's --

00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:19.834
I mean, it's the biggest
responsibility I've ever felt.

00:01:19.834 --> 00:01:23.033
You feel such a responsibility
to him, to his family,

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:25.534
to all the people around
the world who love him so much.

00:01:25.534 --> 00:01:28.000
And so it's terrifying.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.534
But it's also the thing where,
as an actor --

00:01:30.534 --> 00:01:32.901
Like, the actors that I always
looked up to when I was a kid --

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:35.234
My dad always had Turner Classic
Movies in the house.

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:39.334
And so "Raging Bull"
and "East of Eden"

00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:40.968
and "On the Waterfront"
and all those.

00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:43.667
So it was always like,
"What did they do?"

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:45.801
And so it's always been
about the challenge.

00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:48.868
So I just leant into that
as much as I could.

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:51.400
-But then once
you got cast as Elvis,

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.467
do you go like,
"Okay, now I got the gig.

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:54.934
Now it's really hard.
I really got to get to work."

00:01:54.934 --> 00:01:57.501
-Yeah, you feel like you're
about to climb Mount Everest.

00:01:57.501 --> 00:02:00.367
-Yeah. It really is.
And you did it perfectly.

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:01.601
-Oh, thank you.
-I can't even tell you.

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:03.601
You didn't do too much.
It wasn't too little.

00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:05.200
Wait till you see
this performance.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.934
I'm telling you, buddy,
it was a knockout.

00:02:06.934 --> 00:02:10.501
I mean, just even the accents
that Elvis had,

00:02:10.501 --> 00:02:12.467
the way he talked in the '50s,

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:14.334
the way he talked
in the '60s and the '70s,

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.534
like three different Elvises.

00:02:16.534 --> 00:02:18.000
And you sang differently.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.767
And you actually sang
in this movie.

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:21.300
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.267
Well, I'm saying that because...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:24.601
Sorry. But, I mean,
we've had some actors

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:25.834
on this show.

00:02:25.834 --> 00:02:27.634
They play parts in movies,
and they don't sing

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:28.734
and they lip-sync.

00:02:28.734 --> 00:02:31.234
But you can play guitar
and you sang.

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:33.667
And you danced and moved just
like -- It was --

00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:35.400
But, I mean,
how did you do the accents?

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.901
How did you remember
what you were doing?

00:02:36.901 --> 00:02:38.667
-Well, you know,
I mean, for one thing,

00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:41.200
you listen to Elvis speak.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.267
And there's these amazing
archives out there

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:45.000
of every interview he ever gave.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.801
And that was the first thing
that hit me was,

00:02:46.801 --> 00:02:49.300
it's not just
one voice of Elvis.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.100
His voice changed
so much over the years.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.067
And so it's that thing of --

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:55.501
-Could you give us an example?

00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:56.601
I'm sorry to
put you on the spot.

00:02:56.601 --> 00:02:58.634
-I told myself
I wasn't gonna do this.

00:02:58.634 --> 00:03:00.667
[ Cheers and applause ]
Alright, alright.

00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:01.801
-A little bit?
-Alright.

00:03:01.801 --> 00:03:04.534
So, I mean,
I haven't done this at all.

00:03:04.534 --> 00:03:06.467
And it's been a long time,
so take it easy on the Internet.

00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:11.634
But -- Alright, so, like,
an example is,

00:03:11.634 --> 00:03:14.300
when he was 19 and he goes on
the "Louisiana Hayride."

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.200
It's 1954.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.667
And his voice --
You know, he's nervous.

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:20.901
His voice at that time --
You speak a lot faster

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:21.968
when you're nervous.

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:23.367
And, also, he was young.

00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:27.000
And so his voice
at that point was like,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.734
"Well, I'd like to say how happy
we are to be down here.

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:30.234
It's a real honor
for us to be --

00:03:30.234 --> 00:03:32.434
get a chance to appear
on 'Louisiana Hayride.'

00:03:32.434 --> 00:03:33.701
So like that.
-Yep.

00:03:33.701 --> 00:03:37.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.300
And then like
a couple years later, he's 21.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.934
It's 1956.

00:03:41.934 --> 00:03:44.501
And, honestly,
this one I thought of earlier,

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:46.534
because it's how
I feel right now.

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:47.601
It's that thing when
you're not sleeping at night.

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:48.801
You're just buzzing.

00:03:48.801 --> 00:03:51.267
I can't believe --
I've got to take a second.

00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:54.734
I'm on "Jimmy Fallon" talking
about playing Elvis Presley.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:58.467
Like, you're my hero, man.
Like, this is crazy.

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:01.634
It just blows my mind.
I really...

00:04:01.634 --> 00:04:04.834
Like, I just -- Yeah, so,
at this point, he's 21.

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:09.367
And at that point,
he's more like -- He says --

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:11.801
'Cause he's asked, "What are you
thinking about at night?"

00:04:11.801 --> 00:04:15.701
And he says, "Well,
everything has happened to me

00:04:15.701 --> 00:04:19.267
so fast in the last year and a
half till I'm all mixed up.

00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:20.334
You know, I mean,
I can't keep up with

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:21.467
everything that's happening."

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:25.400
-Yeah. It's unbelievable.
-So, there's that.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.534
And then for,
like, a juxtaposition,

00:04:27.534 --> 00:04:29.868
like, we could
fast-forward to '72.

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:31.400
Like, there's this
great interview in '72,

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.701
and one of the
first things he says is,

00:04:35.701 --> 00:04:38.133
"Well --" 'Cause at this point,
his voice goes more forward

00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:39.601
than to his face, you know?

00:04:39.601 --> 00:04:42.467
And, so, then he's like...

00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:47.300
"Well, you know -- Well, we just
came out here from Memphis."

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.434
That sort of thing.
-Yeah, it's just coming out --

00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:50.667
-It comes out of the front of --

00:04:50.667 --> 00:04:52.434
-It's like
three different characters.

00:04:52.434 --> 00:04:55.701
It's such a great love letter
to Elvis and any Elvis fan.

00:04:55.701 --> 00:04:57.167
Dude, ultimate respect.

00:04:57.167 --> 00:04:59.601
And I was reading
some article about this,

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:02.634
and you were saying what kind of
hit home to you is that --

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:03.834
his love for his mother.

00:05:03.834 --> 00:05:06.467
-Yeah, yeah.
-And how he lost his mom.

00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:08.601
-Yeah. He was --
That was the first thing.

00:05:08.601 --> 00:05:09.901
'Cause when I first started
approaching this,

00:05:09.901 --> 00:05:13.234
I think of Elvis
is always 40 feet tall.

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:15.934
He's like --
You can only look up to him.

00:05:15.934 --> 00:05:18.367
And it's hard to --
in the beginning,

00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:21.133
to feel anything
but small compared to him.

00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:24.067
And all those questions --
You know, I'm a shy person.

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:27.467
I feel very --
you know, those moments of

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:30.434
"Am I enough?" and imposter
syndrome and all those things.

00:05:30.434 --> 00:05:32.434
And so it was a process
in the beginning

00:05:32.434 --> 00:05:34.601
of just trying to find
his humanity.

00:05:34.601 --> 00:05:37.934
And I tried many things,
but I watched

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:40.767
this documentary and I learned
that his mom passed away

00:05:40.767 --> 00:05:42.100
when he was 23.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.334
And that's how old I was
when I lost my mom.

00:05:44.334 --> 00:05:46.367
And that hit me like
a freight train,

00:05:46.367 --> 00:05:48.767
because it's
the most human thing,

00:05:48.767 --> 00:05:50.801
like, that grief that you feel.

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:53.968
And I know you've experienced,
you know, that feeling.

00:05:53.968 --> 00:05:55.934
And that's the thing where
it just humanizes somebody,

00:05:55.934 --> 00:05:58.200
and you suddenly
don't feel alone in that.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.367
And so that became the first
key into finding his humanity.

00:06:02.367 --> 00:06:04.901
-Yeah.
You just played it perfect.

00:06:04.901 --> 00:06:06.400
And you got to --

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.968
Obviously,
you had to go to Graceland,

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:11.501
I'm assuming,
and meet Priscilla and --

00:06:11.501 --> 00:06:13.000
-That was so surreal.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.901
The first time I went, it was
about a month after I was cast.

00:06:15.901 --> 00:06:18.901
And Baz and I took this road
trip from Nashville to Memphis.

00:06:18.901 --> 00:06:21.400
And I got to record -- The first
recording studio I ever was in

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:25.133
was RCA, where Elvis recorded
over 250-something songs.

00:06:25.133 --> 00:06:26.434
You know, it was insane.

00:06:26.434 --> 00:06:28.634
And, so,
then we drove to Memphis.

00:06:28.634 --> 00:06:32.133
-I'm freaking out.
I mean, this is unbelievable.

00:06:32.133 --> 00:06:34.834
-Also, I was just in Graceland,
and Angie, who runs the spot,

00:06:34.834 --> 00:06:37.300
she said, "Tell Jimmy,
'cause I know he's a big fan.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.300
Tell him
I will show him everything."

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:40.367
So take you in
the archives, everything.

00:06:40.367 --> 00:06:41.567
-Really?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.501
-'Cause I've not been,
'cause I want to wait for

00:06:43.501 --> 00:06:45.100
the exact right moment
to go to Graceland.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:46.601
-Oh, you'll get all the hookups.

00:06:46.601 --> 00:06:47.667
-Really?
-Yeah, for sure.

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:49.434
-Someone sent
over this photo of you

00:06:49.434 --> 00:06:52.734
in the Jungle Room playing --
Is it Elvis' guitar?

00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:56.033
-Yeah. I still am waiting
for somebody to wake me up.

00:06:56.033 --> 00:06:57.901
-I mean, wait.
So, how did you end up --

00:06:57.901 --> 00:07:01.100
-So, this is -- Like,
they had wanted me, you know,

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.033
for social media or something --
they were like,

00:07:03.033 --> 00:07:05.934
"Well, we'll get you, like,
playing a guitar."

00:07:05.934 --> 00:07:07.067
And they were gonna
just have me play

00:07:07.067 --> 00:07:08.667
some random guitar at one point.

00:07:08.667 --> 00:07:10.000
I kept putting it off

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.934
because I'm not a big
social-media person, as well.

00:07:12.934 --> 00:07:14.267
And then,
when we got to Memphis,

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:15.434
they were like,
"We're making you do it."

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:16.968
And so they got
some other guitar.

00:07:16.968 --> 00:07:20.300
And Angie heard that I was
going to be playing a song.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.000
And she said,
"What guitar are you playing?"

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.534
I said,
"Oh, it's a 1937 Epiphone.

00:07:24.534 --> 00:07:27.167
And she goes,
"Well, I got a '56 Gibson."

00:07:27.167 --> 00:07:28.501
And I realized
what she was saying.

00:07:28.501 --> 00:07:30.400
And this is --
I actually didn't know this.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.367
But that guitar is
the guitar he played in

00:07:33.367 --> 00:07:36.501
"Loving You," "Jailhouse Rock,"
"King Creole."

00:07:36.501 --> 00:07:39.534
It's also the guitar --
Scotty Moore had it redone.

00:07:39.534 --> 00:07:42.133
So it's the same guitar he plays
in "That's the Way It Is."

00:07:42.133 --> 00:07:44.234
It was the same guitar
he plays in Vegas.

00:07:44.234 --> 00:07:45.734
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah.

00:07:45.734 --> 00:07:47.367
And the last person to play it

00:07:47.367 --> 00:07:50.400
in the archives
was Paul McCartney.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.501
-The last person to play it
was Paul McCartney.

00:07:52.501 --> 00:07:54.534
I mean, come on.
What is going on?

00:07:54.534 --> 00:07:57.701
How great is that?
And what was Priscilla like?

00:07:57.701 --> 00:08:00.434
-Oh, man,
she's just a loveliest person.

00:08:00.434 --> 00:08:04.367
And when I first met her,
she said very few words,

00:08:04.367 --> 00:08:06.467
but it was more
looking in her eyes,

00:08:06.467 --> 00:08:08.901
and you realize,
this is the woman

00:08:08.901 --> 00:08:11.367
that Elvis fell in love with
all those years ago.

00:08:11.367 --> 00:08:13.434
And she still loves him
to this day

00:08:13.434 --> 00:08:14.767
and she's the mother
of his only child.

00:08:14.767 --> 00:08:16.934
And it was so surreal.

00:08:16.934 --> 00:08:19.667
And then --
But she said a couple things.

00:08:19.667 --> 00:08:21.734
She said,
"You have big shoes to fill."

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.267
And I said, "I know."

00:08:23.267 --> 00:08:24.567
Just you feel the pressure.

00:08:24.567 --> 00:08:28.634
And then she said -- And then
she gave me this big hug

00:08:28.634 --> 00:08:30.467
and she said,
"You have a lot of support."

00:08:30.467 --> 00:08:32.100
And then that was huge.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:33.901
And then she said, "Have you
been to Graceland yet?"

00:08:33.901 --> 00:08:36.033
I said, "I'm about to go in
for the first time."

00:08:36.033 --> 00:08:38.567
And she said, "I truly believe
that's where his spirit is."

00:08:38.567 --> 00:08:40.033
And then I went over there
for the first time.

00:08:40.033 --> 00:08:42.601
And I spent the whole day
in there by myself.

00:08:42.601 --> 00:08:45.901
And now getting to, you know,
spend time with her

00:08:45.901 --> 00:08:49.601
and be in the Jungle Room
and, like, be with Lisa Marie,

00:08:49.601 --> 00:08:51.534
I just -- It made me feel like
I was a part of the family.

00:08:51.534 --> 00:08:53.100
It was so amazing.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.434
-Are you a dancer?

00:08:54.434 --> 00:08:55.601
I'm sorry to ask this,
because --

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:57.534
-No, no.

00:08:57.534 --> 00:08:59.300
-I was like, I don't know
if I've seen you --

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.367
-No, I wasn't
a singer or a dancer.

00:09:01.367 --> 00:09:03.934
-No. But you definitely
dance in this.

00:09:03.934 --> 00:09:07.334
I mean, you're,
like, a shy person.

00:09:07.334 --> 00:09:08.868
You wouldn't --
Like, at a party --

00:09:08.868 --> 00:09:11.367
-I was always a wallflower.
-Really?

00:09:11.367 --> 00:09:14.100
-Yeah. But that was the thing is
you realize that Elvis wasn't --

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:18.033
He didn't do choreography
in the way that Michael did

00:09:18.033 --> 00:09:19.200
or something like that.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.634
He was -- It was all
the music moving him.

00:09:22.634 --> 00:09:24.467
And, obviously,
there are signature things

00:09:24.467 --> 00:09:28.501
that he did that, you know,
you have to be specific about.

00:09:28.501 --> 00:09:31.334
But it was all about the
way that the music moved him.

00:09:31.334 --> 00:09:34.434
-Could you show me how to move?
-Oh, God.

00:09:34.434 --> 00:09:39.434
-Again, we...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:39.434 --> 00:09:42.133
If you can't --
-Alright, alright, get out here.

00:09:42.133 --> 00:09:45.400
Okay. I haven't done this --
It's been a long time.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:47.334
♪♪

00:09:47.334 --> 00:09:51.701
Alright.
So, this is from the '50s.

00:09:51.701 --> 00:09:54.234
He had this one
that was really fun.

00:09:54.234 --> 00:09:56.167
